  "title": "Erdős Problem #38",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Does there exist $B\\subset\\mathbb{N}$ which is not an additive basis, but is such that for every set $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ of Schnirelmann density $\\alpha$ and every $N$ there exists $b\\in B$ such that $ \\lvert (A\\cup (A+b))\\cap \\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\rvert\\geq (\\alpha+f(\\alpha))N $ where $f(\\alpha)>0$ for $0<\\alpha <1 $?\nThe Schnirelmann density is defined by $ d_s(A) = \\inf_{N\\geq 1}\\frac{\\lvert A\\cap\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\rvert}{N}. $ ",
    "sha256": "3ad0d9ae7b0ccec7bcdabca652228cf10b4175942710f5d0704796bee31ded47",
